# This repository runs with `core.autocrlf=true` and has no repository-root
# `.gitattributes`, so a fresh worktree otherwise receives CRLF and changes
# every byte this crate's gates read. Five sibling crates already carry this
# file — `grapt-api`, `-event`, `-policy`, `-provider` and `-schema` — and
# `grapt-api`'s own header records what its absence cost there: a fresh
# default-config clone reported 107 passed / 1 failed against 108 / 0 on an LF
# one, same commit, same toolchain, same machine.
#
# `grapt-service` had none. R4D-2C is the first lane in this crate whose gates
# are byte-exact rather than line-oriented, so it is the first lane the gap
# could actually redden:
#
#   * `tests/probes/*.stderr` is byte-compared by trybuild and would redden on
#     a CRLF checkout with no content change at all;
#   * `tests/controller_corpus.rs` compares the corpus surfaces it PRODUCES
#     against the committed bytes exactly, so a CRLF checkout would fail the
#     replay for a reason that has nothing to do with the model.
#
# Both defences are here on purpose, and neither is sufficient alone. This
# attribute pins what the gates read; the corpus reader is independently
# newline-agnostic so the gates do not DEPEND on it. A checkout contract that is
# the sole defence fails silently the first time somebody clones with a
# different configuration — and a normalising reader alone would leave the
# byte-compared `.stderr` exposed, because trybuild does its own comparison and
# never consults this crate's readers.
#
# Verified before landing: every one of the 45 tracked files under this crate
# and this lane's corpus already holds LF in the index, so these rules pin the
# CHECKOUT and change no stored content.
src/** text eol=lf
tests/** text eol=lf
mutations/** text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
